WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:08.060
Narrator: Babies cry all the time,

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.750
whether you like it or not.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:14.190
And it takes a very patient
parent to make them stop.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.880
When it comes to TV and movies, however,

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.473
a director may actually want
a baby to cry on command.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:24.460
And this requires hiring someone

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.151
with a special set of skills.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:32.560
That's Elaine Hall.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.180
She's what's known in
Hollywood as a baby wrangler.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.700
Elaine Hall: A baby
wrangler is a interpreter

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:44.880
between what the director
wants a young child to do

00:00:45.747 --> 00:00:47.540
in front of a camera.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.830
Narrator: She's worked with
infants and young children

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:54.140
on a variety of commercials,
TV shows, and movies,

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:57.600
like "The Flintstones,"
"Grace Under Fire,"

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.620
and "Akeelah tnd the Bee."

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:03.540
First off, where do these
crying babies come from?

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.020
Babies, just like adult actors,

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.620
come from casting calls,

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.960
but they're only legally allowed to work

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.100
for four hours a day on set,

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.350
so there's a simple trick used
to extend their camera time.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:20.350
Hall: Very often, when you
see a TV show or a film,

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.430
twins are used to play one role.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.190
Narrator: There's also a
few other rules in place

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:27.550
when using young actors.

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:29.270
Hall: For every set that has a child,

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.710
there's a studio teacher on the set.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:36.710
For infants, there must be a
studio teacher and a nurse.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.050
Narrator: For non-crying scenes,

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:40.820
working with babies and young children

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:42.930
involves making it fun for them.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:44.550
Hall: Everything was made into a game.

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:49.500
I would put on the lens of the camera,

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.160
funny faces on the lens,

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.590
when they needed to
make direct eye contact.

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:55.040
I would be behind the camera

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.480
on every scene, making them laugh.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.930
Narrator: But for crying,
it's a whole different story.

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:01.970
Elaine uses a special technique.

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:04.750
Hall: What I do to get babies to cry is,

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:07.170
I start to cry myself.

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:09.940
For example, I'll go, "Wahhhh!"

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:22.890
And the baby will start to cry.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:27.890
When a baby, even an infant,
hears another baby cry,

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.253
the infant or the baby will
start to cry themselves.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:38.150
This is an example of empathy.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:39.710
It's pretty instantaneous.

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:42.870
Narrator: She used this technique
in "Akeelah And The Bee."

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.400
In this scene, Akeelah's
sister brings her baby

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.893
to the event, and it starts to cry.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:56.780
And her technique has
never failed, so far.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:59.610
Hall: My success rate is 100 percent.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:01.790
Narrator: She says she's
heard of other baby wranglers

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:05.530
using questionable techniques
like placing bad odors

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:09.180
in front of the child
or making a loud noise.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.500
But according to Elaine,
these sorts of methods

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.510
may be harmful to the child,
and should be avoided.

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:17.490
If the baby's face doesn't
need to be shown on camera,

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.933
oftentimes, they'll use a doll instead.

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:27.190
Once it's crying, how do
you get the baby to stop?

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:29.580
Hall: When I stop crying, and
then I'll look at the baby,

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:32.690
and assure the baby
that everything's safe,

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:35.140
I'm okay, she's okay,

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:38.380
then, very quickly, a
baby will stop crying.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:40.480
Narrator: She may also
use rattles and bells

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:42.550
to help, if necessary.

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:45.530
Regardless of the scene,
Elaine says it's important

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:49.040
that the baby spend time
with the actor ahead of time.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.680
Hall: I always have the actor and the baby

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.190
create relationship off camera,

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.950
so that it's not like
you're just plopping a kid

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:00.783
into a stranger's lap.

